Give the generated signal trampolines internal linkage.

The C++ shim rusty-cogs writes for a cog module holds, for each signal method,
a small trampoline whose address travels to Rust as carrier data. Those
trampolines are named `signal_<cog index>_<method>` -- unique within one file,
because a cog's position in its module is what distinguishes them -- and are
emitted inside an anonymous namespace, which the generator's own comment says
is there "so that nothing links against a trampoline".

The anonymous namespace does not achieve that: the definitions are `extern "C"`,
and C language linkage gives a function external linkage and an unmangled name
whatever namespace it sits in. So two cog modules' shims linked into one process
define `signal_0_get_execution_count_stats` twice and the link fails. That is
not an exotic case: a Clockwork process hosts one box, and a box may hold cogs
from more than one module -- the simulated driver and the control-rate cogs of
this repo's own system are exactly that.

`static` is what the anonymous namespace was reaching for. The trampolines are
only ever taken by address within their own translation unit and assigned to
plain C++ function-pointer members, so nothing else about the shim changes; the
calling convention is unaffected on every platform this graph targets.

The upstream fix, written in the wrong repository: every consumer linking two
Rust cog modules into one process needs it until it lands there.

--- a/generator/rusty_cogs/emit_cpp_shim.py
+++ b/generator/rusty_cogs/emit_cpp_shim.py
@@ -742,9 +742,12 @@
     """Write what the carrier's function pointers point at.
 
     Inside this module's own namespace, so that the generated signal API types
-    are reachable unqualified, and inside an anonymous namespace within it, so
-    that nothing links against a trampoline: the addresses reach Rust as carrier
-    data, never as relocations.
+    are reachable unqualified, and inside an anonymous namespace within it, and
+    each one `static`, so that nothing links against a trampoline: the addresses
+    reach Rust as carrier data, never as relocations. The `static` is what makes
+    that true -- a trampoline's name is unique only within its own file, and C
+    language linkage would defeat the anonymous namespace and give two modules'
+    shims colliding definitions the moment they are linked into one process.
 
     Args:
         out: The writer to append to.
@@ -1063,14 +1066,15 @@
     Args:
         out: The writer to append to.
         index: The cog's position in the module, which makes the name unique
-            across the file.
+            across the file. Only across the file, which is why the definition
+            below has internal linkage.
         cog: The cog whose signal API the trampoline calls into.
         method: The method it calls.
     """
     api = names.cpp_signal_api_type_name(cog.name)
     parameters = [f"{type_} {name}" for type_, name in _trampoline_parameters(method)]
     signature = (
-        f'extern "C" {_trampoline_result(method)} {_trampoline_name(index, method)}'
+        f"static {_trampoline_result(method)} {_trampoline_name(index, method)}"
     )
     out.comment(f"Calls `{method.cpp_name}` on the signal API of `{cog.name}`.")
     if len(parameters) == 1:
